Composite layer contents in software, because nothing ever read what a toolkit flushed there.

This port has no GL: CALayerContext answers nil and CARenderer never runs, which is fine for a
view that draws in drawRect: and a silent void for one that PAINTS INTO A BUFFER AND FLUSHES IT AS
LAYER CONTENTS. Qt does exactly that: QCALayerBackingStore renders every widget into an IOSurface
and its flush is layer.contents = surface. The window stayed the clear colour with the entire UI
sitting unread on the layer, centre pixel ffededed, and QT_MAC_WANTS_LAYER=0 does not turn the
path off in this Qt build.

The read side is one hook in displayRectIgnoringOpacity: after drawRect: returns, a view whose
layer has contents composites them into the current context. After, not before, because the
toolkit paints and flushes INSIDE the expose that drawRect delivers, so the freshest frame is
already on the layer at that point. The IOSurface is MESSAGED for its geometry rather than linked,
it being an ObjC object in this port; a CGImageRef contents is drawn directly, recognised by class
because this CG stack does not export CGImageGetTypeID. A flipped view gets the image drawn in
unflipped coordinates, and the data provider retains the surface so the pixels outlive the image
if the layer swaps contents mid draw.

WITH THIS, THE ENTIRE CMAKE UI RENDERS AND RELAYOUTS ON RESIZE: source and build path fields,
preset combo, search row, the cache table with alternating rows, Configure and Generate, the
progress bar and the output pane, at both 1256x684 and 1000x600. Zero faults, zero exceptions.
INTERACTIVE is not yet tested, so CMake is still not claimed for the roster. Task #227.

